    "content": "Charles Robert Redford Jr. (August 18, 1936 – September 16, 2025) was an iconic American actor, director, producer, and founder of the Sundance Film Institute, known for leading roles in Butch Cassidy and the Sundance Kid and The Sting. He won an Oscar for directing Ordinary People (1980) and received the Presidential Medal of Freedom in 2016. Key Details About Robert Redford: Death: Redford passed away at age 89 on September 16, 2025, at his home in Utah. Born: August 18, 1936, in Santa Monica, California. Spouse: He was married to Sibylle Szaggars (2009–2025) and previously to Lola Van Wagenen (1958–1985). Children: He had four children with Lola Van Wagenen: Scott (died in infancy), Shauna, David (died 2020), and Amy. Early Life & Education: Raised in Santa Monica, he was a baseball player in high school and briefly attended the University of Colorado on a baseball scholarship before pursuing art and acting. Career & Legacy: Beyond acting, he founded the Sundance Film Institute and Festival in 1981 to support independent filmmakers. He was a major figure in environmental advocacy and directed notable films including Quiz Show and A River Runs Through It.",
